# `#[topics]`

<SymbolMeta />
<SymbolSignature />

Declares a non-empty enum of outbound typed publications and matching client subscriptions. `#[topic]` on each variant and `#[content]` on templated payload fields are helper syntax consumed and stripped by this macro; they have no standalone meaning.

```rust
use upwell::axum::prelude::*;

#[topics(protocol = Stomp)]
enum Notifications {
    #[topic("/topic/system")]
    System(SystemEvent),

    #[topic("/topic/rooms/{room}")]
    Room {
        room: String,
        #[content]
        event: RoomEvent,
    },
}
```

## Syntax

```text
topics       := #[topics(protocol = Protocol [, codec = Codec])] enum Name { variant, ... }
static        := #[topic("destination")] Variant(Payload)
templated     := #[topic("destination/{hole}")] Variant {
                     hole: Type,
                     #[content] payload: Payload,
                 }
```

`protocol = Protocol` is required and names a messaging protocol. `codec = Codec` is optional; without it, encoding and decoding use `<Protocol as MessagingProtocol>::DefaultCodec`. No other `#[topics]` arguments are accepted.

Every variant has exactly one `#[topic("destination")]`. A static destination is a tuple variant with exactly one payload field. A templated destination is a struct variant with exactly one `#[content]` payload field. Every `{hole}` needs a same-named non-content field, every non-content field must fill a hole, and client subscription methods take those parameters in template order. Templates support `{name}` holes and literal braces escaped as `{{` and `}}`; empty or unmatched braces are errors.

## Generated artifacts and features

Expansion keeps the enum, implements `upwell::axum::Topic` with `destination()` and codec-backed `encode()`, and creates `{Enum}Client<C>` when the macro crate's `client` feature is enabled. The client has `subscribe_<variant>()` methods returning decoded typed subscriptions; static destinations need no arguments and templated destinations take their hole arguments.

The wasm wrapper additionally requires the compatible WebSocket transport feature. It is not emitted for generic topic enums because wasm-bindgen exported types cannot be generic. With wasm TypeScript support, subscription callbacks preserve typed payload names.
